videos.script:
Ask what screen your app runs on, and the honest answer is: you do not know. {{question}}

The same phone turns sideways. A foldable opens into something close to a tablet. A tablet splits in two, and your app gets one half. {{shapes}} So the thing to design for is not a phone or a tablet. It is a window, and its size can change while the app is running. {{window}}

Android sorts windows into size classes by their width in dp: compact, medium, expanded, and two wider ones. iOS calls each axis compact or regular. {{classes}} In this lesson you learn where those lines fall, and what stretches and what stays fixed when a window grows. {{stretch}} Then the planner's course list and course detail become one pane or two. A short program sorts nine example windows into classes and makes that choice for each of them. {{panes}}
